                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "In Elbub, you are a bubble. You move by holding your mouse on other bubbles, following a path that would make Rainbow Road veterans weep, with the end goal of reaching a big bubble. There's no real rhyme or reason for why you are doing this, and while ordinarily that would be fine with me... this game needs more substance to get away with throwing me into a bubble world and leaving me to float. It's not particularly intuitive to play, either. The mouse controls are fiddly at best, and even if right-clicking toggles a \"psychedelic mode\"... it's not really enough to make up for the fact that playing this game just isn't that fun. Holding down on a bubble sends you vaguely in the direction of the one you clicked, but because of... something in the game physics, you easily wind up off course and out of the game world, forcing the level to reload.\n\nIt gets old rapidly.\n\nSo over all, this is certainly a weird title. If you, like me, will try anything once, well... I can't really compare this to anything except for the freakishly niche DS title \"Soul Bubbles.\" It's certainly got that going for it... but on the other hand, it's not even relaxing enough to be a game to wind down with, and I can't justify paying more than a few cents for it as a result (just in my personal opinion!) It's not particularly... fun, and I say this as someone that plays Walking Sims. My standards are low.\n\nOverall I just can't personally recommend this one!\n\n",
